TRAPPING RAIN WATER USING TWO POINTERS (Optimized)

⚠️ You already have Rain Water / Trapping Rain Water — but most students only know prefix-max method.

Here we will solve using Optimal O(1) space Two Pointer Approach (very important in interviews).

🟢 Problem Statement

You are given an array height[] of size n.

Each element represents elevation height.

Return the total units of water trapped after raining.

🔎 Example
Input:
height = [4,2,0,3,2,5]

Output:
9

📊 Visualization
        |
|       |
|   |   |
| | | | |
|_|_|_|_|_
4 2 0 3 2 5


Water trapped = 9 units

🟢 Approach 1 (Brute Force - O(n²)) ❌

For every element:

Find left max

Find right max

Water = min(leftMax, rightMax) - height[i]

Too slow.

🟢 Optimal Approach: TWO POINTER METHOD (O(n) time, O(1) space)
💡 Key Logic

Water trapped at any index depends on:

min(maxLeft, maxRight)


Instead of computing arrays,
we use:

left pointer

right pointer

leftMax

rightMax

🟢 Algorithm Steps

Initialize:

left = 0
right = n - 1
leftMax = 0
rightMax = 0
water = 0


While left < right:

If height[left] < height[right]

If height[left] >= leftMax
→ update leftMax

Else
→ water += leftMax - height[left]

move left++

Else

If height[right] >= rightMax
→ update rightMax

Else
→ water += rightMax - height[right]

move right--

Return water

🟢 WHY THIS WORKS? 🔥 (Interview Explanation)

If height[left] < height[right],
then we are guaranteed that left side is limited by leftMax only.

Because even if right increases, water depends on the minimum boundary.

So we process the smaller side first.

This guarantees correctness.

🟢 Java Code
public class TrappingRainWater {

    public static int trap(int[] height) {

        int left = 0;
        int right = height.length - 1;

        int leftMax = 0;
        int rightMax = 0;

        int water = 0;

        while (left < right) {

            if (height[left] < height[right]) {

                if (height[left] >= leftMax) {
                    leftMax = height[left];
                } else {
                    water += leftMax - height[left];
                }
                left++;

            } else {

                if (height[right] >= rightMax) {
                    rightMax = height[right];
                } else {
                    water += rightMax - height[right];
                }
                right--;
            }
        }

        return water;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] height = {4, 2, 0, 3, 2, 5};
        System.out.println(trap(height));  // Output: 9
    }
}

